{
  "title": "History and Locations of the New York Society of Model Engineers",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "name": "New York Society of Model Engineers (NYSME)",
      "type": "Organization",
      "isolated_context": "The NYSME was originally incorporated in 1926 in New York City. Published records indicate the Society existed as early as 1905. In its early years, it operated as a gentlemen's club of members interested in all types of model building. The organization was formalized and incorporated under the laws of the State of New York in 1926 to obtain a permit for model motor boat races in Central Park. It began construction of its first Model Railroad, \"The Union Connecting,\" at this time. Over twenty years, the Society moved from its original location to two other locations, with each move doubling the size of both the previous location and the model train layout. During WW2, many members were called to service in the Armed Forces, leading to the dismantling of the largest layout when the basement of the Astor Building was requested for the war effort. After the War, an invitation from the Lackawanna Railroad led them to move into its Passenger Terminal in Hoboken, NJ, where they built a magnificent layout based on the Lackawanna Railroad route from Hoboken to Scranton, Pa., which included models from the Hoboken Terminal to the Delaware Water Gap. In the early-1950s, the organization moved to its current location in Carlstadt, New Jersey."
    },
