{
  "title": "Profile of James P. Comer",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "name": "James P. Comer",
      "type": "Person",
      "isolated_context": "James P. Comer, born as James Pierpont Comer on September 25, 1934, in East Chicago, Indiana, is a world's leading child psychiatrist. He currently holds the position of Maurice Falk Professor of Child Psychiatry at the Yale Child Study Center, a role he has held since 1976. Additionally, he serves as an associate dean at the Yale School of Medicine. His work focuses on improving the scholastic performance of children from lower-income and minority backgrounds. He is the author of ten books, including \"Maggie’s American Dream: The Life and Times of a Black Family\" (1988), \"Leave No Child Behind: Preparing Today's Youth for Tomorrow's World\" (2004), and \"What I Learned in School: Reflections on Race, Child Development, and School Reform\" (2009). He has also authored over 150 articles for Parents magazine and more than 300 articles concerning children's health and development and race relations. Dr. Comer was a co-founder and past president of the Black Psychiatrists of America. He has lectured and consulted globally, including in London, Paris, Tokyo, Dakar, Senegal, and Sydney, Australia."
